Drives its own private GC state (`state = []i64(gcWords)`, bound via
`gcBindMemory`/`gcInit` to its own arena) and its own collection driver
(`chanDriveCollection`, called explicitly twice), not the ambient runtime
collector — the two `print`ed scans assert on THAT collector's own counts
(`gcNumObjects`/`gcStatTotalSwept`), which are correct regardless of whether
the ambient (libbitrt) collector ever runs at all.

CORRECTION: "the ambient collector has nothing live to find and legitimately
never fires under BIT_GC=stress" was false. Measured, aarch64-macos, 5 runs
each policy: default `collections=0` every time, `BIT_GC=stress`
`collections=113` every time (denominator: 5/5, zero spread) — matching
stress.bit's own documented scheduler-loop floor
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`). Confirmed default and
`BIT_GC=stress` stdout are byte-identical to `.expected`
(`scan1 6 0 6 6015` / `scan2 0 6` / `collections 2`), so the ambient
collections genuinely run and genuinely do not disturb this program.

Every allocation this program makes goes through the private `g` handle, so
the ambient collector never traces or sweeps any of its objects. The backing
arenas (`arena`, `indexArena`, `heapBlock`) themselves are ordinary managed
`[]i64` slices, though, and this program's own header claims they are "held
live in main for the whole program" — that claim held up empirically here
(byte-identical output under real ambient collection) but is NOT itself
verified by anything this program prints, unlike gcbit's `alive` line or
gcspanblocks'/gcspanfree's/gcspanfreelist's `rooted` field. #4184 tracks
whether that gap is real (the compiler's live-interval-based stack maps
protect a slice only through its LAST textual use, which for `arena` here is
the `gcBindMemory` call itself, not through the private collector's actual
use of it) or is closed by some other runtime guarantee — do not treat this
file as a template for a new private-GC-heap fixture until #4184 resolves.
